---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import { PLAN_YEAR, IRMAA_TAX_YEAR, partB, giveback, usd } from '../../data/medicare-figures';
---

<ToolLayout
  slug="part-b-giveback"
  title={`Part B Giveback Estimator ${PLAN_YEAR} — Premium Reduction`}
  description={`See what a Medicare Advantage Part B giveback is actually worth against the ${PLAN_YEAR} standard premium of ${usd(partB.standardPremium, 2)} — and what it does not reduce.`}
  h1="What's a Part B giveback worth?"
  lede={`Some Medicare Advantage plans pay back part of your Part B premium. It is a real benefit and it is also the single most oversold thing in Medicare marketing. Here is the arithmetic.`}
>
  <form id="gb" novalidate>
    <div class="t-field">
      <label for="gb-amount">Giveback amount the plan advertises <span class="t-unit">/ month</span></label>
      <input type="number" id="gb-amount" name="amount" min="0" max="250" step="0.10"
             inputmode="decimal" value="50" />
      <span class="t-hint">
        A plan cannot give back more than the standard premium of
        {usd(giveback.maxMonthly, 2)} a month.
      </span>
    </div>

    <fieldset class="t-fieldset">
      <legend>Do you pay an IRMAA surcharge?</legend>
      <div class="t-options">
        <label class="t-option"><input type="radio" name="irmaa" value="no" checked /><span><strong>No</strong><em>My {IRMAA_TAX_YEAR} income was below the threshold</em></span></label>
        <label class="t-option"><input type="radio" name="irmaa" value="yes" /><span><strong>Yes</strong><em>I pay more than the standard premium</em></span></label>
        <label class="t-option"><input type="radio" name="irmaa" value="unsure" /><span><strong>Not sure</strong></span></label>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="t-field" id="gb-surcharge-wrap" hidden>
      <label for="gb-surcharge">Your IRMAA surcharge on Part B <span class="t-unit">/ month</span></label>
      <input type="number" id="gb-surcharge" name="surcharge" min="0" max="600" step="0.10"
             inputmode="decimal" value="81.20" />
      <span class="t-hint">
        The amount above {usd(partB.standardPremium, 2)}. Not sure?
        <a href="/tools/irmaa-estimator/">Work it out here</a>.
      </span>
    </div>

    <div class="t-actions">
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn--lg" data-magnetic="0.24">Show me the math</button>
      <button type="reset" class="btn btn--ghost">Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="t-error" id="gb-error" role="alert"></p>
  </form>

  <div class="t-result" id="gb-result" hidden role="region" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Your giveback">
    <p class="t-result__eyebrow">{PLAN_YEAR} effect</p>
    <h2 class="t-result__head" id="gb-head"></h2>
    <div class="t-stats" id="gb-stats"></div>
    <div class="t-result__body" id="gb-body"></div>
    <div class="t-actions">
      <a href="/contact/?intent=30-minute-call" class="btn btn--lg" data-magnetic="0.24">
        Check whether the plan is actually right
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div slot="disclaimer">
    <p>
      Calculated in your browser from {PLAN_YEAR} figures and what you entered. Nothing is
      transmitted or stored.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This shows the premium effect only.</strong> It says nothing about whether the plan
      offering the giveback is a good plan — that depends on its network, its formulary and its
      out-of-pocket maximum. No plan or carrier is named here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="explainer">
    <h2>What a giveback actually is</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Its proper name is the <strong>Part B premium reduction benefit</strong>. Certain Medicare
        Advantage plans use part of their federal payment to cover some or all of your Part B
        premium. It is credited through Social Security, so it appears as a smaller deduction from
        your monthly benefit rather than as a payment from the plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is entirely legitimate. It is also the thing daytime television advertising shouts about
        hardest, which is worth keeping in proportion.
      </p>

      <h3>Three things it does not do</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not reduce your IRMAA surcharge.</strong> The giveback applies to the
          standard premium only. If your income puts you in an IRMAA bracket, that surcharge is
          still owed in full. This is the detail most often left out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not make the plan free.</strong> You still pay whatever the plan charges,
          plus copays as you use care, up to its out-of-pocket maximum.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not travel with you.</strong> Leave the plan and the giveback stops.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Why the trade-off deserves a hard look</h3>
      <p>
        A plan that hands back {usd(50, 2)} a month is giving you {usd(600)} a year. Real money.
        But the plan pays for it somewhere, and it is usually one of three places: a narrower
        network, higher copays, or fewer supplemental benefits.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which means the giveback is only a good deal if the plan clears the checks that matter
        first. In order:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Are your doctors in the network? If not, nothing else matters.</li>
        <li>What is the out-of-pocket maximum? That decides what a bad year costs.</li>
        <li>How does your medication list price on its formulary?</li>
        <li><em>Then</em> compare the giveback.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A {usd(600)} annual giveback on a plan that costs you your cardiologist, or puts a drug on
        a higher tier, is not a saving. More on that in
        <a href="/learn/medicare-advantage-networks/">how Advantage networks work</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Availability is genuinely local</h3>
      <p>
        Giveback plans exist in some counties and not others, and the amounts vary. Availability in
        Maricopa County is not the same as elsewhere in Arizona, and it changes every plan year.
        This site deliberately does not publish plan names or counts — those go stale, and a stale
        number is worse than no number.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you already pay an IRMAA surcharge, the higher-value question is usually not the
        giveback at all — it is whether you can
        <a href="/learn/irmaa-explained/">appeal the surcharge</a>, which for a recent retiree is
        often worth several times more.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</ToolLayout>

<style>
  .t-unit { font-weight: 400; color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.85em; }
</style>

<script>
  import { partB, giveback, usd } from '../../data/medicare-figures';

  const form = document.getElementById('gb') as HTMLFormElement | null;
  if (form) {
    const err = document.getElementById('gb-error')!;
    const result = document.getElementById('gb-result')!;
    const head = document.getElementById('gb-head')!;
    const stats = document.getElementById('gb-stats')!;
    const body = document.getElementById('gb-body')!;
    const surchargeWrap = document.getElementById('gb-surcharge-wrap')!;

    const num = (n: string) => {
      const el = form.elements.namedItem(n) as HTMLInputElement;
      const v = Number(el?.value);
      return Number.isFinite(v) && v >= 0 ? v : 0;
    };

    const syncIrmaa = () => {
      surchargeWrap.hidden =
        (form.elements.namedItem('irmaa') as RadioNodeList).value !== 'yes';
    };
    form.querySelectorAll('input[name="irmaa"]').forEach((r) =>
      r.addEventListener('change', syncIrmaa)
    );
    syncIrmaa();

    form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();

      let amount = num('amount');
      if (amount <= 0) {
        err.textContent = 'Enter the giveback amount the plan advertises.';
        return;
      }

      // A plan cannot give back more than the standard premium.
      const capped = amount > giveback.maxMonthly;
      if (capped) amount = giveback.maxMonthly;
      err.textContent = '';

      const irmaaMode = (form.elements.namedItem('irmaa') as RadioNodeList).value;
      const surcharge = irmaaMode === 'yes' ? num('surcharge') : 0;

      const grossPremium = partB.standardPremium + surcharge;
      const netPremium = Math.max(0, grossPremium - amount);
      const annual = amount * 12;
      const pctOfStandard = (amount / partB.standardPremium) * 100;

      head.textContent =
        amount >= partB.standardPremium
          ? 'That covers your entire standard Part B premium.'
          : `That is ${usd(annual)} a year back in your pocket.`;

      stats.innerHTML = `
        <div class="t-stat">
          <span class="t-stat__value">${usd(grossPremium, 2)}</span>
          <span class="t-stat__label">Part B before giveback</span>
        </div>
        <div class="t-stat">
          <span class="t-stat__value">${usd(netPremium, 2)}</span>
          <span class="t-stat__label">Part B after giveback</span>
        </div>
        <div class="t-stat">
          <span class="t-stat__value">${usd(annual)}</span>
          <span class="t-stat__label">Back per year</span>
        </div>
        <div class="t-stat">
          <span class="t-stat__value">${Math.round(pctOfStandard)}%</span>
          <span class="t-stat__label">Of the standard premium</span>
        </div>
      `;

      const paras: string[] = [];

      paras.push(
        `<p>Your Part B premium goes from <strong>${usd(grossPremium, 2)}</strong> to <strong>${usd(netPremium, 2)}</strong> a month, credited through Social Security as a smaller deduction rather than a payment from the plan. Over twelve months that is <strong>${usd(annual)}</strong>.</p>`
      );

      if (capped) {
        paras.push(
          `<p><strong>Capped at the standard premium.</strong> You entered more than ${usd(giveback.maxMonthly, 2)}, and no plan can give back more than the standard Part B premium — there is nothing above that to reduce. The figures above use the cap. If a plan is advertising more than this, read the offer again carefully.</p>`
        );
      }

      if (surcharge > 0) {
        paras.push(
          `<p><strong>Your IRMAA surcharge is untouched.</strong> The giveback reduces the standard premium only, so the ${usd(surcharge, 2)} a month you pay because of your income is still owed in full — that is ${usd(surcharge * 12)} a year the giveback does not reach. Medicare marketing very rarely mentions this.</p>`
        );
        paras.push(
          `<p>Which points at a bigger opportunity: if you have retired since your IRMAA was set, <a href="/learn/irmaa-explained/">appealing it with form SSA-44</a> can be worth several times more than the giveback. Worth doing that first.</p>`
        );
      } else if (irmaaMode === 'unsure') {
        paras.push(
          `<p><strong>Worth checking whether IRMAA applies to you.</strong> If your income is above the threshold you pay a surcharge on top of the standard premium — and a giveback does not reduce it. The <a href="/tools/irmaa-estimator/">IRMAA estimator</a> will place you in about a minute.</p>`
        );
      }

      paras.push(
        `<p><strong>Now the part that decides whether this is a good deal.</strong> The plan funds that ${usd(annual)} somewhere, and it is normally a narrower network, higher copays, or fewer extras. Before the giveback counts for anything, the plan has to pass three checks: your doctors in-network, an out-of-pocket maximum you can live with, and your medications priced sensibly on its formulary. A giveback on a plan that costs you a specialist is not a saving.</p>`
      );

      paras.push(
        `<p class="t-fineprint">Giveback availability and amounts vary by county and change every plan year. This shows the premium effect only, not whether the plan is right for you.</p>`
      );

      body.innerHTML = paras.join('');
      result.hidden = false;
      result.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'nearest' });
    });

    form.addEventListener('reset', () => {
      result.hidden = true;
      err.textContent = '';
      window.setTimeout(syncIrmaa, 0);
    });
  }
</script>
